          <avActivityHighlights>New snow above approximately 1000 m.</avActivityHighlights>
          <avActivityComment>Natural avalanches are possible as the day progresses. Mostly these are small. Above approximately 2300 m they can be released in the new snow and wind slab layers. &lt;br/&gt; &lt;br/&gt; The wind slabs of the weekend will be covered with new snow and therefore difficult to recognise. Single backcountry tourers can release avalanches in isolated cases. &lt;br/&gt; Adjacent to ridgelines and in gullies and bowls and at the base of rock walls the likelihood of avalanches is higher.</avActivityComment>
          <snowpackStructureComment>10 to 20 cm of snow, and up to 30 cm in some localities, will fall until the evening above approximately 1000 m. &lt;br/&gt; As a consequence of the moderate to strong southeasterly wind, fresh snow drift accumulations will form in the course of the day. &lt;br/&gt; Above approximately 2000 m there are 30 to 60 cm of snow, and even more in some localities. &lt;br/&gt; The snowpack will become in some cases unstable. &lt;br/&gt; Towards its base, the snowpack is moist, in particular below approximately 2500 m. &lt;br/&gt; At low and intermediate altitudes only a little snow is lying. &lt;br/&gt; The Avalanche Warning Service currently has only a small amount of information that has been collected in the field.</snowpackStructureComment>
          <tendencyComment>Northerly wind. The danger will persist.</tendencyComment>
